es contain three integers each: the numbers of cities a i ,ΓÇë b i ( 1ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë a i ,ΓÇë b i ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ), connected by the i -th road and the number of days d i the army spends to go on it ( 1ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë d i ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë10 9 ). The lengths of several roads may coincide.\n\n## Output\n\nPrint on the first line two integers ΓÇö the number of beautiful trees on the most beautiful road and the number of the most beautiful roads. Print on the second line the list of the most beautiful roads in the sorted order by the numbers' increasing. The roads are numbered from 1 to n ΓÇë-ΓÇë1 in the order in which they are given in the input data.\n\nPlease, do not use %lld specificator to write 64-bit integers in C++. It is preferred to use the cout stream (also you may use the %I64d specificator).\n\n## Examples\n\nExample 1:\n```\n2\n2 1 5\n```\n```\n2 1\n1\n```","type":"text"}],"contestId":87,"index":"D","memoryLimit":"256 megabytes","name":"Beautiful Road","problemKey":"87-D","rating":null,"tags":[],"timeLimit":"1 second","url":"https://codeforces.com/contest/87/problem/D"},{"approaches":[{"label":"Approach 1","number":1,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 2","number":2,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 3","number":3,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 4","number":4,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 5","number":5,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 6","number":6,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 7","number":7,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 8","number":8,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 9","number":9,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 10","number":10,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 11","number":11,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 12","number":12,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 13","number":13,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 14","number":14,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 15","number":15,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 16","number":16,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 17","number":17,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 18","number":18,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 19","number":19,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]},{"label":"Approach 20","number":20,"solutions":[{"code":"","fileName":"main.cpp","language":"cpp"}]}],"assets":[],"contentBlocks":[{"sequence":0,"text":"## Problem Statement\n\nA long time ago somewhere in the depths of America existed a powerful tribe governed by the great leader Pinnie-the-Wooh. Once the tribe conquered three Maya cities. Pinnie-the-Wooh grew concerned: there had to be some control over the conquered territories. That's why he appealed to the priests of the supreme god Mogohu-Rea for help.\n\nThe priests conveyed the god's will to him: to control these three cities he should put an idol to Mogohu-Rea ΓÇö that will create a religious field over the cities. However, the idol is so powerful that it can easily drive the people around it mad unless it is balanced by exactly three sacrifice altars, placed one in each city. To balance the idol the altars should be placed so that the center of mass of the system of these three points coincided with the idol. When counting the center of mass consider that all the altars have the same mass.\n\nNow Pinnie-the-Wooh is thinking where to put the idol. He has a list of hills, that are suitable to put an idol there. Help him to identify on which of them you can put an idol without risking to fry off the brains of the cities' population with the religious field.\n\nEach city has a shape of a convex polygon such that no three vertexes lie on a straight line. The cities can intersect. Each altar should be attached to the city through a special ceremony, besides, it must be situated on the city's territory (possibly at the border). Thus, there may be several altars on a city's territory, but exactly one of them will be attached to the city. The altars, the idol and the hills are points on the plane, some of them may coincide.\n\nThe hills are taken into consideration independently from each other, the altars' location for different hills may also be different.\n\n## Input\n\nFirst follow descriptions of the three cities, divided by empty lines. The descriptions are in the following format:\n\nThe first line contains an integer n , which represent the number of the polygon's vertexes ( 3ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë n ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë5┬╖10 4 ). Next n lines contain two integers x i , y i each, they are the coordinates of the polygon's i -th vertex in the counterclockwise order.\n\nAfter the cities' description follows the integer m ( 1ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë m ΓÇëΓëñΓÇë10 5 ), which represents the number of hills. Next m lines each contain two integers x j , y j , they are the coordinates of the j -th hill.\n\nAll the coordinates in the input data do not exceed 5┬╖10 8 in the absolute value.\n\n## Output\n\nFor each hill print on a single line \" YES \" (without the quotes) or \" NO \" (without the quotes), depending on whether the three sacrifice altars can be put to balance the idol or not.\n\n## Examples\n\nExample 1:\n```\n3\n0 0\n1 0\n1 1\n\n4\n8 8\n5 5\n6 4\n8 4\n\n3\n-1 -1\n-3 -1\n-2 -2\n\n5\n0 0\n2 1\n7 1\n1 1\n5 3\n```\n```\nNO\nYES\nNO\nYES\nNO\n```\n\n## Note\n\nFor the hill at (2,ΓÇë1) the altars can be placed at the points (1,ΓÇë0),ΓÇë(7,ΓÇë5),ΓÇë(ΓÇë-ΓÇë2,ΓÇëΓÇë-ΓÇë2) , for the hill at (1,ΓÇë1) ΓÇö at the points (0,ΓÇë0),ΓÇë(6,ΓÇë4),ΓÇë(ΓÇë-ΓÇë3,ΓÇëΓÇë-ΓÇë1) . Many other groups of three points can do the trick. There are no suitable points for other hills.","type":"text"}],"contestId":87,"index":"E","memoryLimit":"256 megabytes","name":"Mogohu-Rea Idol","problemKey":"87-E","rating":null,"tags":[],"timeLimit":"3 seconds","url":"https://codeforces.com/contest/87/problem/E"}],"schemaVersion":1}
